  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1031 PM CDT SAT AUG 29 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery paints a fairly active picture across the\ncountry this evening with an upper trough off to our northeast\nand a stout upper level ridge anchored across the Four Corners\nregion. A bit closer to home, we can see a lingering H5 vort max\ncan be seen across the north Gulf coast, which is helping to aid\nin some scattered convection to our south. At the surface, a weak\nsurface low can be analyzed across the Northern Gulf, about\n125 miles south of Pensacola. This feature will slowly drift west\nover the coming days. The NHC currently has a low (20%) chance of\ntropical development over the next 7 days. While most of Central\nAlabama is likely to remain dry, we will see low (~20%) chances\nfor showers and storms each afternoon as deeper moisture wraps\naround the surface low and spreads across our southern areas. At\nthis time, we are not expecting much in the way of severe weather\nas wind shear will be minimal. However, instability will be\nsufficient enough to support a stronger storm or two as we reach\npeak heating.\n\nAs we head through the work week, the upper ridge will expand\neast. This will help to steer the tropical low to the west across\nthe Gulf. Drier air and increased subsidence will help maintain\nrain-free conditions across the northern half of the area. We\nlook to continue with low chances for diurnally driven convection\nacross our southern areas where moisture remains elevated closer\nto the coast.\n\nA warming trend kicks in over the coming days as temperatures\nclimb back into the mid to upper 90s by midweek. Heat Advisories\nare likely to return by Wednesday as heat indices settle into the\n105-107 degree range.\n\n95/Castillo",
